// The user interface: the components most applications are built from, and
// the vocabulary they share.
//
// What is here is the portable core -- a `button` means the same thing on
// the web, on a phone, in a desktop toolkit and in a terminal, and a
// platform that renders it differently still renders the same declaration.
// Specialised surfaces live in packages under this one, so that a program
// pays for a drawing canvas or a document viewer only by importing it:
//
//	sngl:ui/draw    a canvas and the 2D shapes it hosts
//
// `Style`, the style enums and the event payloads sit here rather than in a
// package of their own because every component in every one of those
// packages refers to them; they are the vocabulary, not a separate concern.
//
// Nothing here is in scope until it is imported. `import . "sngl:ui"` writes
// its declarations unqualified; `import <alias> "sngl:ui"` qualifies them,
// and is also how a platform overrides a component (`component <alias>.vbox`).
